                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1] Experienced on the Oculus Rift with Touch Controllers [/h1]\n\n[i] Note: I played with 2 sensors in standing mode while seated perfectly fine. So seated or standing mode doesn't really matter [/i]\n\nThis is about a 20 minute VR cinematic experience, with only a few interactions. I'm going to give a tepid recommendation. If you liked the first Firebird La Peri then likely you will also enjoy this VR experience, but it is not for everyone. First of all, although there is music and dance, it really isn't the focus this time around. The ballet dancing happens near the end and only for a short while.\n\nMost of the focus is on the story and sculpting. The Unifinished story I found less exciting than La Peri, but it was better directed. It was just a little too by the numbers. I knew where the story was going and it wasn't an original idea. The best part was definitely the sculpting interactions. I just wish they had more depth. Also, more interactions like these throughout the story would have made the story come to life more.\n\nThe visuals are top notch. The sounds are good. The dance number was a joy to watch, but it was very brief and I wanted more. I did not like how the sculptures were not anatomically correct. I think they wanted to make this story more approachable to families and children so maybe that was the reason for the decision. But it really betrays the art, which is ironically the theme of this series. I really doubt any artist would make these sculptures and make then anatomically incorrect. Art should not be censored. We're grownups. At least offer a censored version for those with kids if that really was a concern.\n\nRate 6/10. Only recommended for those into these types of VR experiences. See below.\n\nhttps://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=1372448347",
